<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abalgamash</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">23rd century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/23rd-century-bce">23rd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Abalgamash was a king of Marhashi, a land east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elam">Elam</a> in south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, in the 23rd century BCE. Inscriptions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rimush">Rimush</a>, king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akkad">Akkad</a>, record that he defeated Abalgamash and his allies in a great battle and brought back booty, including stone vessels, which he dedicated in Mesopotamian temples.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abalgamash.jpg" alt="Abalgamash" width="320" height="407" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Photograph: 0x010C Transcription:F. THUREAU-DANGIN (3 January 1872 in Paris – 24 January 1944 in Paris) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AO_5476_(front,_with_transcription).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
